ed in package.json for completeness but are not strictly required for the application to run. The auto‑update stub will only activate if electron-updater is present at runtime. To fully utilise these features, install the listed dependencies within the electron_bootstrap directory ( npm install ) and run the build script ( npm run dist ) on a machine with internet connectivity and the appropriate build tooling. Milestone 1 – AES‑256‑GCM Vault Encryption (PBKDF2 100k) Implemented a new vault encryption engine using AES‑256‑GCM with a PBKDF2‑derived key. Encryption now uses a 16‑byte random salt, a 12‑byte IV and derives a 256‑bit key using 100 000 iterations of SHA‑256. Data are encrypted with the Node crypto API via the vaultAPI exposed by preload.js . The resulting vault strings encode salt:iv:cipher:tag as base64. Fallback logic remains for browser‑only builds using Web Crypto. Manual testing confirmed that saving and loading vault files using this method round‑trips the state and console log without error. See T‑01 above for details. Milestone 2 – QR Camera Unlock + Image Fallback Added live QR scanning using the optional html5‑qrcode library. A hidden founder panel now shows a Scan Ritual button that opens the camera, scans for a QR secret and promotes the user to Founder when the secret matches FOUNDER‑KEY‑777 or contains the word “founder”. When the library is unavailable or scanning fails, the module falls back to scanning a selected image file via Html5Qrcode.scanFile() . As • • • • • • • • 3 <PARSED TEXT FOR PAGE: 4 / 6> a final fallback, the code inspects the uploaded file’s filename for the keywords founder or unlock . Unlock events append a timeline entry, award XP and display an alert. Manual tests verified that the founder status updates and advanced controls unlock when a correct QR or filename is provided. Milestone 3 – LLM Token‑Stream Handler Implemented server‑sent event streaming for the LLM chat endpoint. A new IPC handler llm-chat￾stream in main.js posts a chat request with stream:true to the local Ollama model and reads each line of the response as it arrives. Partial JSON payloads are parsed and forwarded to the renderer via the llm‑stream‑data channel. In bindings.js the send handler attaches event listeners to append partial tokens to the console in real time. Errors are forwarded via llm‑stream‑error and displayed in red. If streaming is unavailable, the code falls back to the standard chat call. Testing using the llama3 model confirmed that responses arrive token by token and update the console without blocking the UI. Milestone 4 – Secure Preload Bridge (NeuralVault Path Sanitisation) Introduced a dedicated vault directory under the user’s home ( ~/NeuralVault ). All file selections and reads/writes now resolve relative to this directory. The resolveVaultPath() helper in main.js validates that resolved paths never escape the directory. The read-file , write-file and save-file-dialog handlers now use this resolver. Selecting or saving a file returns only the basename to the renderer so that untrusted paths cannot be injected. Directory creation occurs on startup. These changes harden the preload bridge without restricting legitimate vault operations. Milestone 5 – Auto‑Update Feed & Event Logging Updated package.json build configuration to set the publish provider to generic with URL https:// example.com/updates . In main.js , the app now listens to electron‑updater events ( update‑available , update‑downloaded , error ) and forwards them to the renderer via auto‑update‑event . The renderer appends these events to the console and timeline, allowing the user to monitor update checks. The updater automatically checks for updates when the app is packaged. Manual testing on a packaged build confirmed that events are forwarded correctly when the updater is present and silently skipped when it is not. Milestone 6 – Tier & Rank FX Enhanced the visual and audio feedback for tier changes and XP rank advances